  "verdict": {
    "iid_bite_erasure": "bit-slicing is a real 2.4-5.5x multiplier; on HND0 rate 0.50/p0.30 reaches 8.47 (1t) and 11.20 (2t), rate 0.30/p0.50 reaches 5.80 (2t). rate 0.40/p0.40 needs block512 L16 for recovery 0.999168 and its HND0 throughput is pending T5.2.",
    "recovery": "p0.30/r0.50 block256 L16 0.999821 (1M, lower 0.999783); p0.40/r0.40 block512 L16 0.999168 (1M, lower 0.999090); p0.50/r0.30 block256 L16 0.999862 (160k, lower 0.999763). All three p have a viable recovery point.",
    "bottleneck": "independent codeword schedules make a 64-bit mask carry only ~2 useful bits on i.i.d. erasures; the wave event count, not XOR throughput or AVX width, is the limit. With a shared (share-level) erasure mask every mask is full and the decoder reaches 158-260 MB/s locally, 12.5-12.8x.",
    "max_achievable_iid_hnd0": "8.47 MB/s 1t / 11.20 MB/s 2t at rate 0.50/p0.30; 5.80 MB/s 2t at rate 0.30/p0.50; rate 0.40/p0.40 block512 pending.",
    "pending": "T5.2 owns HND0. After T5.2 fixes the announce mask, run the p0.40/r0.40 block512 L16 2-thread 5-round A/B first; then the 5-round 2-thread confirmation for the other points."
